\subsection{CPU Cost Across Retrieval Parameters}
\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{comparison_all.pdf}
\caption{Five-method total CPU comparison. Rows vary $k$, $m$, and the composed per-query quota budget $\varepsilon$. The three clean methods do not use $\varepsilon$ and remain runtime references in its row. Positions are equally spaced, including the descending budget values. Noisy methods share the calibration in (\ref{eq:calibration}).}
\label{fig:comparison}
\end{figure*}

At the default point, FedSeq reduces CPU relative to DANN by 3.12$\times$ and 4.40$\times$ on DNA and Symbol IID, and by 15.80$\times$ and 23.30$\times$ on their Non-IID partitions. Bound construction, sorting, and frontier work are included. Increasing $k$ raises the required prefix lengths and weakens local stopping thresholds. Changing $m$ keeps $N$ fixed but changes shard sizes and the distribution of local requests; this is why total verification need not stay constant when more providers share the same records.

Composition-only filtering retains reordered strings within a family; order evidence reduces their verification. At specialized providers, even a positive quota of one can scan an irrelevant shard. Frontier evidence lets the broker omit that search. The ablation separates these costs.

\subsection{Noise, Computation, and Accuracy}
At $\varepsilon=1$, the DANN$^*$/FedSeq$^*$ CPU ratios are 1.02$\times$, 1.33$\times$, 1.23$\times$, 1.24$\times$, in the same setting order as above. DNA IID is close to a tie: the paired mean CPU difference is 0.70 ms, with a 95\% query-bootstrap interval [-1.06, 2.77] ms. This interval includes zero, so this point does not establish a stable runtime advantage.

Conditional sensitivity makes the second-round noise rate small. Positive padding then moves many requests toward $k$, weakening omission. Smaller budgets add work where clean search stops early, but little at an already fully scanned shard. Noise generation is timed.

DANN$^*$ refines using its clean allocation map; FedSeq$^*$ certifies the actual perturbed first prefix. Its accuracy therefore reflects the full workflow, not geometric noise alone.

We distinguish stable-key mismatches from distance-top-$k$ errors: the latter compare cardinality and sorted oracle distances while allowing boundary ties. Every mismatch is independently reconstructed using recorded quotas and exhaustive distances, without broker allocation or noise code. Timing retains all failures.
\input{table_accuracy}
Across the synthetic sweeps, DANN$^*$ has 644 strict mismatches and 363 distance errors in 11,400 runs; FedSeq$^*$ has 258 and 81, respectively. These are observed error counts, not mean recall or a theoretical accuracy guarantee. All 67,200 clean runs in the synthetic studies match the stable-key oracle.

\subsection{Module Ablation}
\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{ablation_all.pdf}
\caption{Default-point ablation. Top: separate additions of cold scheduling and order filtering to DANN, followed by their combination. Bottom: calibrated DANN$^*$, its order-filter extension, frontier allocation with Laplace floors one and zero, and geometric floor-zero FedSeq$^*$. All noisy variants use the same two rates; Laplace/geometric comparisons also include their shifts and rounding rules.}
\label{fig:ablation}
\end{figure*}

Under IID, order filtering reduces exact distance evaluations from 2,000 to 487.74 on DNA and 297.38 on Symbol. Cold-only scheduling retains 2,000 evaluations; the combined algorithm has the same verification counts as order-only search. The within-provider order ambiguity accounts for the gain here.

Under Non-IID, cold-only scheduling retains 2,000 evaluations at the query family, while order-only search performs 6,308.90 on DNA and 6,164.08 on Symbol. Their combination needs only 308.90 and 164.08. Six irrelevant providers each avoid 1,000 evaluations. Local filtering then reduces work at the remaining providers, illustrating the bound--quota interaction in Proposition~\ref{prop:coupling}.

The large clean gains do not come from integer noise. Padding often revives unselected providers: (\ref{eq:zero-prob}) predicts 5.64 positive first requests among six zero-origin providers at default. No perturbation distribution guarantees faster retrieval.

\subsection{Heterogeneity and Independent Generation}
\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=.9\textwidth]{robustness_all.pdf}
\caption{Robustness on three preselected population seeds. Left: five levels of family concentration in the swap generator. Right: a separate insertion/deletion/substitution generator at three levels. Lines average seed means; shading is their minimum--maximum range, not a confidence interval. DANN+Bigram uses a standard two-gram count filter with unchanged positive allocation.}
\label{fig:robustness}
\end{figure*}

Figure~\ref{fig:robustness} changes composition at balanced sizes. The response is not monotonic: at $\rho=.75$, positive prefixes can exceed small relevant-family slices and require other-family verification. At $\rho=1$, six irrelevant providers are omitted. The benefit depends on sufficient exclusion evidence rather than a generic Non-IID label.

The edit generator does not preserve character histograms and allows variable record lengths. Across its frozen seed/partition cases, DANN/FedSeq CPU ratios range from 3.05$\times$ to 18.78$\times$; DANN+Bigram/FedSeq ratios range from 1.43$\times$ to 18.58$\times$. This comparison tests a stronger established local filter without changing the retrieval kernel or introducing an index. Its scope is the specified scan controls, not all indexed string-search methods. Table~\ref{tab:accuracy} also retains the noisy edit-generator errors.

\input{table_unstructured}
The unclustered control removes centers and family structure. All four methods evaluate all 8,000 records per query: neither the order bound nor the certificate creates useful pruning. FedSeq averages 181.60 ms versus 179.56 ms for DANN. This control exposes the extra filtering cost when the bounds cannot eliminate verification, and limits the performance claim to workloads with exploitable sequence or provider structure.

