\subsection{Real Nucleotide Sequences}
\label{sec:real}
\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{real_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Rfam confirmation on 50 held-out queries, three repeats. Columns vary $k$, $m$, and $\varepsilon$; rows use mixed and family-specialized ownership. Three clean curves in the budget column reuse their default CPU measurements. All perturbed runs, including errors, remain in the averages.}
\label{fig:real}
\end{figure*}
On Rfam, the default DANN/FedSeq CPU ratios are 7.37$\times$ (95\% query-bootstrap interval 5.75--9.69$\times$) under IID and 8.16$\times$ (95\% query-bootstrap interval 6.04--11.30$\times$) under specialization. Figure~\ref{fig:real} shows the parameter sweeps.

Under IID, Baseline, DANN, and FedSeq average 916.91, 749.06, and 101.67 ms. Verification falls from 10620.48 to 1102.30; FedSeq omits 0.00 providers in both rounds on average. The DANN+Bigram control takes 750.82 ms. The DANN$^*$/FedSeq$^*$ CPU ratio is 3.67$\times$.

Under Non-IID, Baseline, DANN, and FedSeq average 1127.31, 1023.82, and 125.53 ms. Verification falls from 11302.00 to 1292.72; FedSeq omits 3.70 providers in both rounds on average. The DANN+Bigram control takes 1069.25 ms. The DANN$^*$/FedSeq$^*$ CPU ratio is 2.62$\times$.

The matched ablation separates the mechanisms: under IID, cold-only, order-only, and combined search evaluate 10623.28, 1102.30, 1102.30 exact distances; under specialization the counts are 11161.00, 3084.12, 1292.72. Combining the modules removes 58.08\% of order-only verification under specialization and reduces CPU by 2.11$\times$ (95\% interval 1.67--2.75). Cold-only scheduling barely reduces verification: stronger frontier evidence enables omission. All 10,500 clean Rfam runs match the stable-key oracle. Table~\ref{tab:accuracy} includes the noisy Rfam errors.

Padding leaves only 0.02 mean omissions in FedSeq$^*$ at default, explaining part of the smaller noisy gain. Other pilots, including the unsuccessful Splice case, are retained in the artifacts.
